{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 TLM JSFX\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 JSFX effects and instruments for REAPER by Thomas LeRoy Meier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Source, issue tracker and a few extra video processors that are not distributed through ReaPack are on GitHub.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Retro Codec Suite\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Emulations of the audio compression formats used by 1990s game consoles, using the real predictor and step tables from each format.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Codecs\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab N64 VADPCM - Vector-Adaptive Differential PCM\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IMA ADPCM - 89-value step table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab 4-bit PCM\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab PSX ADPCM - PlayStation filter coefficients\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Apple 1-bit - delta-sigma modulation\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Controls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Sample Rate - 8 kHz to project rate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Bit Depth - 1 to 16 bits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Saturation - soft saturation before encoding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Artifacts - scales the intensity of the codec's own error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mix, Output - dry/wet and output trim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Anti-Aliasing - optional pre-filter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Lo-Fi Filter - character filter matched to the target rate\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hold Shift while dragging Sample Rate or Bit Depth for continuous values between the notched presets. Double-click any slider to reset it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These codecs alias, and downsampling is done without a decimation filter, because that is what the originals did. If you want the artifacts without the aliasing, turn on Anti-Aliasing. If you want it clean, this is the wrong plugin.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Seed Delay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A 12-tap delay where the tap times are generated rather than dialled in. An algorithm proposes a set of timings, and you edit the result by hand on the grid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Algorithms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Seed Random - unseeded random spread\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Quantum Shuffle - clustered probabilistic spacing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Perlin Noise - smooth correlated variation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Spiral Wave - accelerating or decelerating sweeps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Chromatic - chromatic scale intervals\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Harmonic - harmonic series\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Fibonacci - golden ratio spacing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab L-System - recursive fractal patterns\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Grid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Click a square to enable or disable that tap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Click and drag across VOL or PAN to draw an envelope\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Shift-click to toggle modulation on a tap\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Ctrl-click to reset a control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab REGEN re-rolls the current algorithm\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Notes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Feedback tops out at 0.99 rather than 1.0. At exactly unity the loop neither decays nor settles, and because the taps sum coherently at low frequencies it grows without bound instead of sustaining.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Dirt and Moonlight/Sunlight are non-linear. With both at zero the delay path is linear and will ring longer at high feedback than you might expect.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Cross Modulator\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Five ways for one signal to modulate another, each available with either an external modulator or an internal oscillator. Modulation can be applied to the whole signal or to a selected frequency band only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Routing - read this first\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This plugin has four inputs. The carrier is channels 1-2, the modulator is channels 3-4.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The "x Osc" modes use the built-in oscillator and need no routing at all\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The "x Ext" modes need a send from another track into channels 3-4 of this one\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If an "x Ext" mode is silent or does nothing, the send is missing. Set the Modulator Source parameter to Main In 1-2 to modulate a signal with itself instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Ring - classic ring modulation, carrier suppressed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab AM - amplitude modulation, carrier stays present alongside the sidebands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Phase FM - the modulator displaces a short delay line\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Envelope - a follower on the modulator gates the band, giving rhythm without pitch artifacts. A slow internal oscillator gives tremolo\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Filter FM - audio-rate modulation of the band filter's cutoff\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Controls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Intensity - modulation depth\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Osc Rate - internal oscillator frequency, 20 Hz to 5 kHz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Max FM Depth - delay range for the FM modes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Modulated Band - restrict modulation to a lowpass, bandpass or highpass region, or the full band\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Band Freq, Band Q - the band to modulate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Output Trim\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Ring and FM modes produce inharmonic sidebands by design. Output level varies a lot between modes; use Output Trim.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Cat Ears\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Cochlear stereocilia distortion. A saturation curve taken from the mechanoelectrical transduction of stereocilia in the inner ear, rather than from a circuit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Boltzmann function used here describes how far a hair bundle has to bend before its ion channels open. The curve is asymmetric and its shape depends on level, so it behaves differently on transients than on sustained material. Based on the model in Peterson & Heil, 2020.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Controls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input Gain - how hard the curve is driven\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Drive - saturation amount\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Asymmetry - bias between the two directions of the curve, which sets how much even-order content is produced\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Output Trim\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Wet/Dry\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not oversampled. High Drive on bright material will alias. Use it on the way in, or on a duplicated track, rather than across a full mix.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 MonoDrum\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A monophonic percussion synth whose partials are placed by whole-number frequency ratios instead of by an inharmonicity control. It takes MIDI in and has no audio input, so it goes on an empty track.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Real drums have inharmonic partials. Placing them at just ratios instead gives something that reads as pitched percussion, which is the point rather than a limitation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Sound sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Tuned partials with per-partial ratio, level and decay\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A noise channel with its own envelope and bandpass\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A short click transient\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Structure presets\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Several presets are tunings rather than timbres - the tuning is the patch. Spiral of Fifths is twelve Pythagorean steps. Euler Genus 105 is the divisor lattice of 3x5x7, octave reduced. Otonal Nine is harmonics 8 through 16 as a chord.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pitch follows MIDI note, or set Mode to Hz Override to pin it to a fixed frequency. Trigger Mode switches between Gate, which holds while a note is held, and One-Shot, which always plays the full envelope. Velocity can be routed to decay and to pitch envelope depth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Auto-Normalize Partials keeps the level roughly constant as you add partials. Turn it off if you want adding partials to get louder.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Tap Timing Utility\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tap along with something and read the interval back in the units other plugins ask for. Intended for setting a delay or an LFO by ear when you know how it should feel but not what number it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Reads out\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Milliseconds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Hertz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Samples at the current project rate\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Nearest beat division against project tempo\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs28 Usage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Choose Mouse or MIDI input, then tap the button or play notes. The running average steadies over several taps, so keep tapping rather than trying to land two perfect ones. Audio Click gives you something to tap against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measurement only. It does not process audio and can sit anywhere in a chain.\par}
}
